We compare five techniques for Gardner $5\times5$ minichess, a game that is a proven draw: alpha-beta search with a hand-crafted evaluation, UCT Monte-Carlo tree search, the same search with an evaluation learned by TDLeaf($\lambda$) self-play (reinforcement learning), the same search with an evaluation evolved by a genetic algorithm (GA), and an AlphaZero-style network trained by self-play (reinforcement learning). All agents run on one C++ engine validated against Fairy-Stockfish and are compared at equal compute. At 400 nodes or simulations per move, the hand-crafted, TDLeaf and GA evaluations are within <<spread>> Elo of each other, far above UCT MCTS (<<elo_M>>) and above the AlphaZero-style agent (<<elo_Z>>, 95\% interval [<<lo_Z>>, <<hi_Z>>]). Starting from equal piece values and using only game results, TDLeaf gains <<td_gain>> Elo; the GA reaches the same level from random weights. One simulation of the network agent costs about <<cost_ratio>> alpha-beta nodes, and at equal wall-clock time it scores <<eqt_score>> against alpha-beta. The ranking agrees with play against Fairy-Stockfish at fixed depths and with per-move evaluation loss. An earlier version of our harness let both sides share one transposition table; the external checks exposed it, and we report what it had distorted. Code, data and an interactive board are released.
